            {
              "id": "bb677138-ebce-3db6-98d9-8bdd35671433",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The code does not provide any checks to ensure that the source and destination do not overlap, and dataflow analysis suggests that such overlap is feasible. Additionally, there are no guards to ensure that either the source or destination pointers are non-null, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior or crashes. The operation uses a generic memory copy function, and the size calculation is based on a variable, which may not guarantee safety. These factors indicate a real risk that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the memory copy, add explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. This can be done by comparing the addresses and the size of the regions. If overlap is possible, handle the case appropriately (e.g., use a temporary buffer or a safe copy function).\n\n```cpp\nif (data_.get() != nullptr && vec.data() != nullptr) {\n    auto dst = static_cast<const char*>(data_.get());\n    auto src = static_cast<const char*>(vec.data());\n    size_t bytes = size_ * sizeof(T);\n    if (src + bytes <= dst || dst + bytes <= src) {\n        qu->memcpy(data_.get(), vec.data(), bytes);\n    } else {\n        // Handle overlap safely, e.g., use a temporary buffer or memmove\n    }\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible, use a function that is safe for overlapping regions, such as `memmove`, instead of `memcpy`. This ensures correct behavior even if the source and destination overlap.\n\n```cpp\nif (data_.get() != nullptr && vec.data() != nullptr) {\n    std::memmove(data_.get(), vec.data(), size_ * sizeof(T));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
