            {
              "id": "23fd80c3-9ed4-30a4-acb4-4e685d5a5209",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as a 'UB Risk' (undefined behavior risk) and the verdict is 'GENUINE'. The code copies 8 bytes from a string literal to a destination buffer using a memory copy operation. There are no explicit checks to ensure that the source pointer is not null, and the analysis could not confirm that the source is always valid. The destination is likely safe (not a pointer-based member), but the source could potentially be null, which would result in undefined behavior. The absence of a guard or explicit null check for the source pointer increases the risk. The count argument is a literal value (8), and there is no evidence that the length is derived from the destination's capacity, which means there is no built-in protection against buffer overflows. The overlap risk is low, but the main concern is the potential for a null pointer dereference.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source pointer is not null. This can be done by adding an explicit check before the call:\n\n```cpp\nif (u8\"Infinity\" != nullptr) {\n    std::memcpy(result + sign, u8\"Infinity\", 8);\n}\n```\n\nAlternatively, if the source is always a string literal (as in this case), it is guaranteed to be non-null by the C++ standard. However, if the analysis cannot guarantee this, adding a check is a safe defensive programming practice. Also, ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold 8 bytes starting from the offset `result + sign` to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the destination buffer size is not guaranteed, add a check to ensure that there is enough space before copying:\n\n```cpp\n// Assume result is a char array and sign is an offset\nsize_t buffer_size = /* actual size of result buffer */;\nif ((sign + 8) <= buffer_size && u8\"Infinity\" != nullptr) {\n    std::memcpy(result + sign, u8\"Infinity\", 8);\n}\n```\n\nThis prevents both null pointer dereference and buffer overflow.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
