            {
              "id": "99b37686-cfff-36b6-81b7-2494d8e4a492",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null, and there are no obvious null checks present before the memory copy operation. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', reinforcing the assessment. The destination is a pointer, and there is no evidence that its capacity is checked or that it is guaranteed to be non-null. The source is a string literal, but the analysis conservatively cannot resolve its base, so it may be null in some edge cases. There are no explicit guards or null-terminations after the copy. These factors together indicate a real risk of undefined behavior if either pointer is null at runtime.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore calling the memory copy operation, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are not null. This can be done by adding explicit checks before the call:\n\n```cpp\nif (result != nullptr && u8\"NaN\" != nullptr) {\n    std::memcpy(result, u8\"NaN\", 3);\n}\n```\n\nThis prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference. If either pointer can be null, handle the error or provide a fallback mechanism.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the destination pointer is expected to always be valid, document this assumption and consider using assertions in debug builds to catch programming errors early:\n\n```cpp\nassert(result != nullptr);\nstd::memcpy(result, u8\"NaN\", 3);\n```\n\nThis will help catch null pointer issues during development and testing.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
